# PWA Conversion Implementation Plan (Final)

This plan details the steps required to convert the existing SMash Badminton application into a Progressive Web App (PWA). This plan includes the implementation of seamless update prompts, offline database persistence, and strict security measures.

## User Review Required

> [!IMPORTANT]
> **Security & HTTPS:** Service Workers (the engine behind PWAs) have a strict security requirement: they will **only** run on secure `HTTPS` connections (or `localhost` for testing). Because Service Workers can intercept network requests, this prevents "Man-in-the-Middle" (MITM) attacks and ensures nobody can hijack your app's code. Since we are hosting on Firebase, HTTPS is automatically enforced, fulfilling this critical security standard.

> [!WARNING]
> **Cache Security:** We will implement strict cache versioning. The Service Worker will only cache standard UI files (HTML, CSS, JS, Images). We will **never** cache sensitive user data or authentication tokens in the Service Worker cache. All sensitive data will be handled securely by Firebase's encrypted IndexedDB offline persistence.

## Proposed Changes

### 1. PWA Configuration
#### [NEW] [manifest.json](file:///c:/Users/Admin/Documents/POC/Badminton/manifest.json)
Create the Web App Manifest defining the app's identity, colors, and behavior.
- **App Name & Short Name:** `SMash`
- **Start URL:** `/index.html`
- **Display Mode:** `standalone` (removes the browser URL bar for a native app feel).
- **Theme Color:** `#0f172a` (matches your dark theme).
- **Icons:** We will configure the manifest to use your existing `logo.png`. Modern browsers will automatically scale this existing logo for use on the Android Home Screen and App Launcher.

### 2. Service Worker & Caching (Secure & Stale-While-Revalidate)
#### [NEW] [service-worker.js](file:///c:/Users/Admin/Documents/POC/Badminton/service-worker.js)
Create a highly secure Service Worker script:
- **Cache Versioning:** Define a cache name (e.g., `smash-cache-v1`) to easily clear out old files when updates are pushed.
- **Install Event:** Pre-cache the "App Shell" (the HTML, CSS, and JS files).
- **Fetch Strategy:** Implement a "Stale-While-Revalidate" strategy. It serves the cached UI instantly for speed, but secretly checks the network for updates in the background.
- **Update Activation:** Add logic to listen for the `skipWaiting` message. This allows the new Service Worker to take over instantly when the user clicks the "Update Available" prompt.

### 3. Core App Integrations & Features
#### [MODIFY] [shared.js](file:///c:/Users/Admin/Documents/POC/Badminton/js/shared.js)
Update the core shared script:
- **Service Worker Registration:** Register the Service Worker securely.
- **New Version Detection:** Add event listeners to detect when a new Service Worker is waiting to install.
- **Update UI Prompt:** Add a toast notification that pops up saying *"A new version is available. Click to refresh."* When clicked, it sends the `skipWaiting` signal to the Service Worker and reloads the page.
- **Enable Firestore Offline Persistence:** Add `enableIndexedDbPersistence(db)` to the Firebase initialization. This ensures all database queries (Members, Programs, Enquiries) are securely cached locally, enabling full offline read/write capabilities (with auto-sync when internet is restored).

#### [MODIFY] HTML Files
For every HTML file (`index.html`, `dashboard.html`, `members.html`, `programs.html`, `schedule.html`, `settings.html`), inject the necessary PWA metadata in the `<head>`:
- `<link rel="manifest" href="/manifest.json">`
- `<meta name="theme-color" content="#0f172a">`
- `<link rel="apple-touch-icon" href="/logo.png">`

## Verification Plan

### Automated Tests
- The Service Worker must not throw any syntax or registration errors in the console.

### Manual Verification
1. **PWA Installability:** Open Chrome Developer Tools -> Application -> Manifest to verify it detects the PWA configuration and icons without errors.
2. **Offline Mode:** Disconnect the internet, refresh the page, and verify the app still loads instantly and displays Firestore data from the local cache.
3. **Update Flow:** Simulate an update by manually bumping the cache version in `service-worker.js`, reload the page, and verify the "New version available" toast prompt appears and works when clicked.
